A separate `safe` array keeps the board values untouched during DFS. I only start searches from border `O` cells. After that, an `O` with `safe[row][col] == false` belongs to a surrounded region.

For example, a border `O` connected to three inner `O` cells marks all four positions as safe. An isolated inner `O` is not marked and gets flipped.

An empty matrix is checked before reading `mat[0]`, which also covers the no-cell case.

```java
class Solution {
    private int rows;
    private int cols;
    private boolean[][] safe;

    private void markSafe(char[][] mat, int row, int col) {
        if (row < 0 || row >= rows || col < 0 || col >= cols ||
            safe[row][col] || mat[row][col] != 'O') {
            return;
        }

        safe[row][col] = true;
        markSafe(mat, row - 1, col);
        markSafe(mat, row + 1, col);
        markSafe(mat, row, col - 1);
        markSafe(mat, row, col + 1);
    }

    public char[][] fill(char[][] mat) {
        if (mat == null || mat.length == 0 || mat[0].length == 0) {
            return mat;
        }

        rows = mat.length;
        cols = mat[0].length;
        safe = new boolean[rows][cols];

        for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
            if (mat[0][col] == 'O') {
                markSafe(mat, 0, col);
            }
            if (mat[rows - 1][col] == 'O') {
                markSafe(mat, rows - 1, col);
            }
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            if (mat[row][0] == 'O') {
                markSafe(mat, row, 0);
            }
            if (mat[row][cols - 1] == 'O') {
                markSafe(mat, row, cols - 1);
            }
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                if (mat[row][col] == 'O' && !safe[row][col]) {
                    mat[row][col] = 'X';
                }
            }
        }

        return mat;
    }
}
```

The `safe` matrix uses extra memory, and recursive DFS also uses stack space. Both time and space complexity are `O(rows * cols)`.

Here the cells are treated as connected nodes. I add one extra node called `outside`. Every border `O` is joined to it, and neighboring `O` cells are joined together. At the end, an `O` not connected to `outside` is enclosed and becomes `X`.

Dry run: if an inner group of four `O` cells has no path to the border, its group root differs from the outside root, so all four cells are flipped. A group touching even one border cell shares the outside root and stays unchanged.

```python
class DisjointSet:
    def __init__(self, size):
        self.parent = list(range(size))
        self.rank = [0] * size

    def find(self, value):
        while self.parent[value] != value:
            self.parent[value] = self.parent[self.parent[value]]
            value = self.parent[value]
        return value

    def union(self, first, second):
        root_first = self.find(first)
        root_second = self.find(second)

        if root_first == root_second:
            return

        if self.rank[root_first] < self.rank[root_second]:
            self.parent[root_first] = root_second
        elif self.rank[root_first] > self.rank[root_second]:
            self.parent[root_second] = root_first
        else:
            self.parent[root_second] = root_first
            self.rank[root_first] += 1


class Solution:
    def fill(self, mat):
        if not mat or not mat[0]:
            return mat

        rows = len(mat)
        cols = len(mat[0])
        outside = rows * cols
        dsu = DisjointSet(outside + 1)
        directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]

        for row in range(rows):
            for col in range(cols):
                if mat[row][col] != 'O':
                    continue

                current = row * cols + col

                if row == 0 or row == rows - 1 or col == 0 or col == cols - 1:
                    dsu.union(current, outside)

                for row_change, col_change in directions:
                    next_row = row + row_change
                    next_col = col + col_change

                    if (0 <= next_row < rows and 0 <= next_col < cols and
                            mat[next_row][next_col] == 'O'):
                        neighbor = next_row * cols + next_col
                        dsu.union(current, neighbor)

        outside_root = dsu.find(outside)

        for row in range(rows):
            for col in range(cols):
                if mat[row][col] == 'O':
                    current = row * cols + col
                    if dsu.find(current) != outside_root:
                        mat[row][col] = 'X'

        return mat
```

The empty-board check avoids indexing errors, and a one-row board leaves all `O` cells safe because they are all on the border. Union-find uses `O(rows * cols)` time here and `O(rows * cols)` extra space.

The safe cells are the `O` cells connected to the border. I start DFS from those cells and change them to `S` temporarily. Any `O` left later is enclosed, so it becomes `X`. Finally, `S` changes back to `O`.

Dry run on `[[X,X,X],[X,O,X],[X,X,X]]`: the center `O` is never reached from a border, so the final board changes it to `X`.

```cpp
class Solution{
private:
    void markSafe(vector<vector<char>>& mat, int row, int col) {
        int rows = mat.size();
        int cols = mat[0].size();

        if (row < 0 || row >= rows || col < 0 || col >= cols ||
            mat[row][col] != 'O') {
            return;
        }

        mat[row][col] = 'S';
        markSafe(mat, row - 1, col);
        markSafe(mat, row + 1, col);
        markSafe(mat, row, col - 1);
        markSafe(mat, row, col + 1);
    }

public:
    vector<vector<char>> fill(vector<vector<char>> mat) {
        if (mat.empty() || mat[0].empty()) {
            return mat;
        }

        int rows = mat.size();
        int cols = mat[0].size();

        for (int col = 0; col < cols; ++col) {
            markSafe(mat, 0, col);
            markSafe(mat, rows - 1, col);
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; ++row) {
            markSafe(mat, row, 0);
            markSafe(mat, row, cols - 1);
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; ++row) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; ++col) {
                if (mat[row][col] == 'O') {
                    mat[row][col] = 'X';
                } else if (mat[row][col] == 'S') {
                    mat[row][col] = 'O';
                }
            }
        }

        return mat;
    }
};
```

The input is changed while processing, but the required result is returned as a new vector value. Time complexity is `O(rows * cols)`. The recursion stack can use `O(rows * cols)` space in the worst case.

I used a queue instead of recursion here. All border `O` cells are placed in the queue first, then their connected `O` neighbors are marked with `#`. The remaining `O` cells cannot reach the edge and are changed to `X`.

The board may have only one row or one column. In that case every `O` is on the border, so none of them should be flipped.

```python
from collections import deque

class Solution:
    def fill(self, mat):
        if not mat or not mat[0]:
            return mat

        rows = len(mat)
        cols = len(mat[0])
        queue = deque()

        def add_if_open(row, col):
            if 0 <= row < rows and 0 <= col < cols and mat[row][col] == 'O':
                mat[row][col] = '#'
                queue.append((row, col))

        for row in range(rows):
            add_if_open(row, 0)
            add_if_open(row, cols - 1)

        for col in range(cols):
            add_if_open(0, col)
            add_if_open(rows - 1, col)

        directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]

        while queue:
            row, col = queue.popleft()

            for row_change, col_change in directions:
                add_if_open(row + row_change, col + col_change)

        for row in range(rows):
            for col in range(cols):
                if mat[row][col] == 'O':
                    mat[row][col] = 'X'
                elif mat[row][col] == '#':
                    mat[row][col] = 'O'

        return mat
```

This changes the matrix during the search and then restores the safe cells. Time complexity is `O(rows * cols)`, and the queue can hold `O(rows * cols)` cells.

This version explores one full `O` component at a time. While collecting its cells, it records whether any cell touches the border. If the answer is no, all collected cells are changed to `X`; otherwise they stay as `O`.

The input array is modified directly and returned afterward. This also handles a board made entirely of `X`, since no component is started.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    private int rows;
    private int cols;
    private char[][] mat;
    private List<(int row, int col)> component;
    private bool touchesBorder;

    private void Explore(int row, int col) {
        if (row < 0 || row >= rows || col < 0 || col >= cols ||
            mat[row][col] != 'O') {
            return;
        }

        mat[row][col] = 'V';
        component.Add((row, col));

        if (row == 0 || row == rows - 1 || col == 0 || col == cols - 1) {
            touchesBorder = true;
        }

        Explore(row - 1, col);
        Explore(row + 1, col);
        Explore(row, col - 1);
        Explore(row, col + 1);
    }

    public char[][] Fill(char[][] mat) {
        if (mat == null || mat.Length == 0 || mat[0].Length == 0) {
            return mat;
        }

        this.mat = mat;
        rows = mat.Length;
        cols = mat[0].Length;

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                if (mat[row][col] != 'O') {
                    continue;
                }

                component = new List<(int row, int col)>();
                touchesBorder = false;
                Explore(row, col);

                foreach (var cell in component) {
                    mat[cell.row][cell.col] = touchesBorder ? 'O' : 'X';
                }
            }
        }

        return mat;
    }
}
```

A single-cell board is an edge case because that cell is automatically on the border, so an `O` remains unchanged. Each cell is explored once, giving `O(rows * cols)` time. The stored component and recursion stack together can take `O(rows * cols)` space.

## Intuition
Iterate over boundaries and push them into the queue whenever we find a 'O' and then later mark their surroundings

